<https://example.org/dmo/id/complication/hypoglycaemia> a dmo:Complication ;
    rdfs:label "Hypoglycaemia" ;
    dmo:affectedOrgan "Systemic" ;
    dmo:complicationCode "hypoglycaemia" ;
    dmo:definition "Variation in the inpatient management of hyperglycaemia and hypoglycaemia in people receiving enteral feeding may slow patient recovery and potentially result in further complications such as poor wound healing or super-added infection" ;
    dmo:isEmergency "true"^^xsd:boolean ;
    dmo:mondoCode "MONDO:0005147" ;
    dmo:name "Hypoglycaemia" ;
    dmo:onsetPattern "Acute" ;
    dmo:evidenceQuote "Variation in the inpatient management of hyperglycaemia and hypoglycaemia in people receiving enteral feeding may slow patient recovery and potentially result in further complications such as poor wound healing or super-added infection" ;
    dmo:evidenceQuote "At 10am the following morning the patient was found to be clammy and drowsy – capillary blood glucose was 1.9 mmol/L. This was a result of receiving feed specific insulin despite the feed being stopped." ;
    dmo:evidenceQuote "On a busy ward this is often overlooked or delayed resulting in hypoglycaemia." ;
    dmo:evidenceQuote "Follow local VRIII protocols in order to avoid hypoglycaemia and achieve target glucose range 6-12 mmol/L." ;
    dmo:evidenceQuote "Ward teams need to be aware of the risk of hypoglycaemia if the feed is stopped." ;
    dmo:evidenceQuote "Managing hypoglycaemia in an appropriate and timely manner" ;
    dmo:evidenceQuote "If the feed is interrupted and any feed related insulin is still active there will be an increased risk of hypoglycaemia." ;
    dmo:evidenceQuote "If capillary blood glucose <4 mmol/L or persistently>12 mmol/L on two consecutive occasions or evidence of ketonaemia/ketonuria then inform the DIT or on-call medical team" ;
    dmo:evidenceQuote "in the event of a single episode of hypoglycaemia (capillary blood glucose <4 mmol/L), then a review should take place to identify causal factors and the diabetes management regimen should be reviewed." ;
    dmo:evidenceQuote "Any capillary blood glucose less than 4.0mmol/L should be treated in line with the local hypoglycaemia policy and the patients regularly prescribed diabetes treatment reviewed." ;
    dmo:evidenceQuote "Review the insulin regimen and the insulin dose administered prior to the hypoglycaemic event" ;
    dmo:evidenceQuote "Any episodes of hypoglycaemia should be treated, and then trigger an insulin dose and timing review" ;
    dmo:evidenceQuote "Any episodes of hypoglycaemia should trigger an insulin dose and timing review" ;
    prov:wasDerivedFrom <https://example.org/dmo/id/guidelineSource/jbds_05_enteral_feeding_guideline_april_2024> .

